WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.759 --> 00:00:11.570
Death is inevitable...at least for the individual.

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:16.140
Whether you’re getting consumed, or your
environment poisons you, or you fall

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:20.100
apart as time marches on, we die.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:26.000
But our genes, fellow traveler, our genes
often survive.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.090
We living things...we reproduce.

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:37.010
And while humans, with our experience of
plants and animals, are of course most familiar

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:45.239
with sexual reproduction, that is absolutely
not the only way it can be done.

00:00:45.239 --> 00:00:50.649
By this point on your journey, you should
already know that nothing is usual in the

00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:58.379
microcosmos.

00:00:58.379 --> 00:01:06.460
Most unicellular reproduction is asexual,
meaning one organism, on its own, can reproduce itself.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:10.140
The most common form of asexual cellular reproduction, and the one you are most likely to

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:14.850
know about, in the microcosmos, is binary fission.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:19.320
One organism simply divides into two.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:25.230
Unicellular organisms of course do not have
genders, but by custom, the dividing cell

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:33.230
is called the “mother” while the cells
formed after division are called “daughters.”

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:39.680
This is a Heliozoa, an amoeboid cell with
stiff arms called axopodia radiating from

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:42.190
its spherical body.

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:47.590
Here it undergoes binary fission to create
two daughter cells.

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:54.610
Watch as these two future daughters move in opposing directions with the help of axopodia

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:59.940
until the cytoplasmic bridge that was formed
between them is finally cut off.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:17.780
Now, which of these two cells is the original
and which is the offspring?

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:22.579
In the microcosmos, that’s not always how
it works.

00:02:22.579 --> 00:02:30.300
Both of these Heliozoa are daughters, and
the mother has ceased existing...either that,

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.080
or mother is now...both of them.

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:41.060
And, if that is the case, every single-celled
organism is, itself, the same organism that

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:48.139
has continued surviving since the very first
cell reproduced itself billions of years ago.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:53.340
But, like, we don’t want to hurt your brain
too early in the episode, so it’s probably

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.700
best not to think about it too much.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:03.819
The process of binary fission happens fairly
quickly, all things considered, but of course

00:03:03.819 --> 00:03:07.680
we have to speed up the tape a bit for you to enjoy it.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.480
Here a photosynthetic flagellate called Euglena deses is dividing.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:19.560
The nucleus, however, has already divided, and each of these halves has its own control

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:26.370
center, even though the cytoplasm is still joined, which makes you wonder...are these two separate

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:27.370
organisms now?

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:30.870
Or are they only individuals once their
membranes separate?

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:40.760
Now, we’re pretty sure this organism is
Cyphoderia, but it’s a little hard to be

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:43.970
sure, especially during the reproduction process.

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:49.430
But it’s definitey some kind of Testate
Ameoba, which are ameoba that build shells

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:55.099
for themselves to live in, and that protect
them from predators and, just, the world,

00:03:55.099 --> 00:03:57.249
which can be a rough place.

00:03:57.249 --> 00:04:03.279
You might think that they, like snails or
clams, build these shells around themselves after

00:04:03.279 --> 00:04:07.720
they are born, and that is true from some
Testate Ameobas.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:09.909
But not for this one.

00:04:09.909 --> 00:04:16.030
During cell division, Cyphoderia actually
builds a second shell and then squirts new

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:21.600
cell material into that shell to create
its daughters.

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:35.200
Ciliates like Paramecium and Stentor actually
have two types of nuclei; macronucleus and

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:36.940
a micronucleus.

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:42.841
The macronucleus controls the non-reproductive
cell functions, like eating, movement, and

00:04:42.841 --> 00:04:48.230
digestion and the micronucleus is necessary for
reproduction.

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:52.660
These nuclei actually divide differently during
reproduction.

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:59.500
And for certain cilliates, the micro-nucleus
plays a truly bizarre role...but to talk about

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:04.270
that, we need to move away from asexual reproduction.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:07.410
Which, so far, is all we’ve talked about.

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:10.160
And it’s fine, we guess.

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:16.750
But in all of these cases each daughter cell
is a clone of the mother.

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:23.320
In sexual reproduction, genes can flow much
more easily through populations, which has

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:26.280
a huge evolutionary advantage.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:32.600
But sexual reproduction in the microcosmos
is not like sexual reproduction for the rest

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:33.620
of us.

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:43.080
Conjugation requires two cells.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:48.160
First two ciliates become attached to each
other by their oral surface.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:53.560
Micronuclei in the ciliates then divide several
times in each cell.

00:05:53.570 --> 00:06:02.131
And then one micronucleus from each cell moves
into the other cell and fuses with the micronucleus

00:06:02.131 --> 00:06:03.220
there.

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:08.980
The macronuclei of both cells then dissolves into the cytoplasm.

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:13.360
Each cell now has a brand new genome.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:21.400
It is as if you gave someone a kiss, and walked
away a genetically different person.

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:32.020
There are no children...the gene transfer
happens to the existing organism.

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:38.800
Now, after separation, the cells produce new
micro and macro-nuclei and then reproduce

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:44.781
asexually until each daughter cell has the
correct number of macro and mirco nuclei for

00:06:44.781 --> 00:06:46.560
their species.

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:52.590
Conjugation is thus considered a sexual phenomenon
which is immediately followed by repeated

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:54.310
asexual reproduction.

00:06:54.310 --> 00:07:01.210
This can be hard to get your brain around
because, to us, the exchange of genetic information

00:07:01.210 --> 00:07:05.400
is so deeply tied with the creation of new
offspring.

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:12.660
But in the microcosmos, you can see, genetic
exchange is possible without reproduction.

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:24.840
If I were to tell you that this is only the
very surface of the iceberg of complexity

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:33.270
of reproduction in the microcosmos, I hope that, by this point, you would believe me, because it is.

00:07:33.270 --> 00:07:39.170
These beautiful bags of chemicals have to
continue existing, and they have done so elegantly

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:43.850
using strategies that to us might seem counter-intuitive,

00:07:43.850 --> 00:07:51.190
but are nevertheless the simplest and most
effective ways for keeping their genes alive.

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:55.960
And it’s hard to fault them for it...it’s
been working for them for billions of years

00:07:55.960 --> 00:08:01.990
while you and I, fellow traveller, have only
been here for a blink.

00:08:01.990 --> 00:08:08.440
So, thank you for coming on this journey with us as we explore the unseen world, that surrounds us.

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:15.000
If you want to see more from our master of
microscopes, James, check out Jam and Germs

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:19.640
on instagram, and if you want to see more
from us, there’s always a subscribe button

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:22.220
somewhere nearby.

